sum='I. His First Flight: a young seagull is afraid to fly though his brothers and sister have flown. His family scold and ignore him; he stays alone on a ledge, hungry. His mother picks up a piece of fish and flies towards him, stopping just short. Maddened by hunger, he dives at the fish, falls into space, and in terror spreads his wings – and flies. He lands on the green sea and his family praise him. II. Black Aeroplane: a pilot flying an old Dakota from Paris to England at night, dreaming of breakfast at home, enters huge black storm clouds. His compass and instruments fail; fuel is low. A mysterious black aeroplane without lights appears and guides him to the runway. When he asks the control tower about the other plane, the woman says there was no other aeroplane in the sky that night. Who helped him remains a mystery. How to Tell Wild Animals (Carolyn Wells) – a humorous poem describing how to identify the Asian lion, Bengal tiger, leopard, bear, hyena, crocodile and chameleon. The Ball Poem (John Berryman) – a boy loses his ball into the harbour; the poet says he must learn the "epistemology of loss" – how to bear loss and stand up.',
